  "articleBody": "It's super common for businesses to have an FAQ page, but are those questions and answers truly working for your AI visibility? Often, existing FAQs are thin, outdated, or just not formatted in a way that AI systems can easily use. AI doesn't just skim; it needs clarity and structure to recognize your business as a credible source.\n\nMany sites use heavy CMS themes (think WordPress, Wix, Squarespace) that can slow down or even prevent AI crawlers from accessing and understanding your content effectively. Plus, if your FAQs aren't targeting the *real* questions people are asking on platforms like Quora, Reddit, or even Google Search Console's \"unanswered questions,\" you're missing a huge opportunity.\n\nFor AI to cite you, your content needs \"fact density,\" clarity, and semantic relevance, all wrapped up in rich JSON-LD schema. It's a different ballgame than just having text on a page. Without this, your FAQs, no matter how well-written for humans, might just be invisible to the very systems determining who gets cited in AI Overviews or voice search.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n\nOne big mistake is treating your FAQ section as an afterthought. Don't just list a few generic questions; actively research what your audience is truly asking on various platforms. Another error is neglecting schema markup—without it, AI systems struggle to understand the question-and-answer structure. Using outdated information, having inconsistent answers across your site, or failing to update your FAQs when your services evolve are also common pitfalls that hurt your AI visibility. And finally, relying solely on dynamic, JavaScript-heavy pages for your FAQs can severely hinder AI crawlability, so always prioritize clean, static HTML where possible.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
